{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>But if YHWH create a creation.</b> He used the language of בריאה, which denotes bringing something into being from nothing, and this comes to teach that the wonder of this great miracle would be like the wonder of the world's creation. Now it would seem that Scripture should have said ואם בריאה יברא אלהים, after the pattern of בראשית ברא אלהים, for this is what Moshe asked — that the attribute of judgment be stretched out against them, as it says, ופצתה האדמה את פיה ובלעה אותם וגו׳. And the reason he mentioned the particular Name is that such was Moshe's practice throughout the entire Torah, that he would mention only the particular Name in his speech, because his prophecy and his mission were solely through the particular Name; and so he said above, זאת תדעון כי ה׳ שלחני — for from the attribute called זאת they would know this day that he is the emissary of the particular Name. And because the sin of these sinners was exceedingly grave, and they had deeply corrupted the roots of the Torah, uprooting the foundations like one who digs deep and cuts the root of the tree, and they set themselves against the prophecy of Moshe, who prophesied in the particular Name — therefore he said, ואם בריאה יברא ה׳, that is: if a new creation and a widely-publicized wonder YHWH, blessed be He, is destined to perform before all Israel at one of the times, as He promised me at Sinai (Exodus 34): נגד כל עמך אעשה נפלאות אשר לא נבראו — יברא ה׳ עתה, for now the hour has arrived, and עת לעשות לה׳ הפרו תורתו — it is a time to act for YHWH, for they have violated His Torah; and it is not fitting that this vengeance be carried out through an angel, but only through the particular Name, in the manner written regarding the plague of the firstborn (Exodus 12): ועבר ה׳ לנגוף את מצרים — and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded regarding that plague: not through an angel, and not through a seraph, and not through an emissary, but the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself in His glory. And afterwards he said, וידעתם — he mentioned the language of knowledge both at the beginning of his words and at the end of his words, for concerning Israel he spoke these words. And it is known that just as, had Israel not been struck through the plague of the firstborn, there would have been a profanation of the Name, as I explained there in its place, so too had this punishment not befallen Korah and his assembly, the crown of the Torah would have fallen, and the Name of Heaven would have been profaned."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:20.519288260Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}